  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 217 AM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nUpper level ridging persists late week into next week. This will \ncontinue to favor a warmer, drier pattern through the holiday \nweekend into next week. Temperatures are expected to remain above \nseasonal for early September. For reference, the first week of \nSeptember the average high drops from 85 to 83 degrees. However, \ncurrent forecast highs remain in the upper 80s to mid 90s, with \npotential for upper 90s across southwest Nebraska. The cooler highs, \nin the upper 80s to lower 90s, are generally expected to remain \nacross northern Nebraska and remain consistent with the 75th \npercentile compared to climatology. So, while these highs are warmer \nthan usual, these are not too unusual. Further to the south, where \nhighs remain in the 90s, highs remain consistent with the 90th \npercentile compared to local climatology, which is less usual. By \nearly next week, some uncertainty begins to build into the forecast, \nthough. GEFS spaghetti plots suggest most solutions still favor \nupper level ridging to continue across the region, however, some of \nthe more progressive solutions begin to track an upper level trough \nacross the Northern Plains. If these solutions come to fruition, \nthen we could see the upper level ridge begin to break down early \nweek, potentially bringing slightly cooler highs, yet still \nremaining above seasonal averages. \n\nThe upper level ridging is also expected to help limit precipitation \npotential late week and through the holiday weekend. Ensemble \nguidance continues to remain pessimistic on precipitation chances, \ngenerally keep a less than 10 percent chance of exceeding one tenth \nof an inch of precipitation through the weekend. However, ensembles \ndo still suggest around a 20 to 40 percent chance for light \nrainfall, exceeding one hundredth of an inch. Deterministic guidance \nsuggests a few shortwaves tracking through the upper level flow, \nthough given the forecast positions, would expect this to keep \nsurface lows across the Dakotas. With this in mind, cannot \ncompletely rule out some slight chances for very light \nprecipitation, but not expecting any wetting precipitation through \nthe weekend. For what it is worth, latest NBM guidance does keep \nsome slight chance PoPs, generally less than 20 percent, each \nevening this weekend. However, based off the ensemble guidance, \nwould expect any precipitation that may occur will be very\nlight.",
